{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011CfbHFgNrC6N1uKZfT8kd6", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Here's a regex for ISO 8601 calendar dates in `YYYY-MM-DD` format:\n\n```regex\n^\\d{4}-(0[1-9]|1[0-2])-(0[1-9]|[12]\\d|3[01])$\n```\n\nHow it breaks down:\n- `\\d{4}` is the four-digit year.\n- `(0[1-9]|1[0-2])` is the month, from 01 to 12.\n- `(0[1-9]|[12]\\d|3[01])` is the day, from 01 to 31.\n- `^` and `$` require the whole string to be the date. Use `\\b` instead of `^`/`$` if you want to find dates inside longer text.\n\nIt matches `2026-10-01` and rejects `2026-13-01`, `2026-10-32` and `26-10-01`.\n\n**Limitation:** A regex only checks the format, not whether the date exists. This pattern still accepts `2026-02-30` and `2025-02-29`. If invalid dates need to be rejected, run a real date parse after the regex match. For example, in Python use `datetime.date.fromisoformat(s)`. In JavaScript, check the input against `new Date(s).toISOString().slice(0, 10)`."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 6684, "cache_read_input_tokens": 11903, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 6684}, "output_tokens": 8, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "not_available"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "65aabce0-6e6e-4595-a795-5080df31081d", "uuid": "cc9eb840-e475-423a-8021-de280e13dabe", "timestamp": "2026-10-01T09:28:49.570Z", "request_id": "req_011CfbHFg2WnU5ogN6YEApLR"}
